{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And they came to Marah, etc.</b> HaShem brought before them the matter of the waters of Marah in order to test them again, to see whether they would be worthy of receiving the Torah. For our Rabbis of blessed memory already disagreed (Mekhilta Beshalah [Vayissa] parashah 1) concerning what is written, ״וַיּוֹרֵהוּ ה׳ עֵץ״ (and HaShem showed him a tree): some say it was an olive tree, some say it was a willow, some say oleander; Rabbi Shimon bar Yohai said: He showed him a matter from the Torah, as it is written (Proverbs 3:18) ״עֵץ חַיִּים הִיא״ — she is a tree of life. And the explanation of the matter is that the Torah at its beginning is bitter, and it is called ״תּוּשִׁיָּה״ (firm wisdom), which wears out a person's strength, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'Why is the Torah likened to an olive? Just as this olive — its beginning is bitter and its end is sweet, so too are the words of Torah.' And the commandments too were not given for pleasure, as it is written: ״׳וְתַגֵּד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל׳ — things hard as sinews.׳ For illness of the soul is like illness of the body: just as most bodily illnesses are healed by taking bitter herbs, and if the patient does not trust the physician he will refuse to accept them, so too with illnesses of the soul — for they had become ill through acquiring false beliefs that they acquired in Egypt — the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to heal them through receiving the Torah, which is likened to an olive, for its beginning is bitter like the olive. And there is reason to suspect that perhaps Israel might ponder in their hearts, saying: 'How is it possible to heal a bitter thing with a bitter thing?' Therefore HaShem brought them to a test, and brought before them the matter of the waters of Marah, which were sweetened by a bitter thing. And by this ״נִסָּה אוֹתָם הָאֱלֹהִים״ — Elohim tested them: if they would believe that Moses did this thing by divine power, then they would also believe that through the Torah the bitterness of the soul would be sweetened. And if they were to say that Moses did this thing by some incantation, they would not be worthy of receiving the Torah. Therefore it says ״וַיּוֹרֵהוּ ה׳ עֵץ״, and it should have said ״וַיַּרְאֵהוּ״ (and He showed him) — rather, ״וַיּוֹרֵהוּ״ (and He instructed him) means that He taught him the way of the Torah, as it is written (Proverbs 4:4) ״וַיֹּרֵנִי וַיֹּאמֶר לִי יִתְמָךְ דְּבָרַי לִבֶּךָ שְׁמֹר מִצְוֹתַי וֶחְיֵה״ — and He instructed me and said to me, Let your heart hold fast my words, keep my commandments and live. And He commanded him to take a tree, to serve as a model and parable of the Torah, which is called ״עֵץ חַיִּים״ — a tree of life. And according to the one who says it was an olive, it fits well as a parable for the words of Torah, whose beginning is bitter — like this olive, whose beginning is bitter and whose end is sweet. And concerning this it says ״שָׁם שָׂם לוֹ חֹק וּמִשְׁפָּט וְשָׁם נִסָּהוּ״ — there He set for him a statute and an ordinance, and there He tested him — and this test is whether they would be worthy of receiving the Torah." "And once Israel believed that Moses did that entire great deed by divine power, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, delivered to them there a few commandments: Sabbath, and the red heifer, and civil laws, and honoring father and mother. For these commandments have a relationship with this deed, for through believing in this miracle they will also believe in the creation of the world anew, which the Sabbath testifies to — for this miracle is close to creation from nothing, for the sweetening of these waters came into being from nothing: for the tree that was cast there was also bitter, and if so the waters were not sweetened except by the word of HaShem. And honoring father and mother has a likeness to the Sabbath, for not without reason were these two commandments placed adjacent in the first and second sets of tablets, and in the portion of Kedoshim (Leviticus 19:3) ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ וְאֶת שַׁבְּתֹתַי תִּשְׁמֹרוּ״ — every man, your mother and father you shall revere, and My Sabbaths you shall keep. And it seems probable to me to say regarding this, that it comes to teach that there are three partners in a person: the Holy One, blessed be He, and his father and his mother; therefore He commanded to honor all three together. For honoring the Sabbath is honoring the Holy One, blessed be He, for since the Sabbath is a marvel testifying to the creation of this world, it follows as well, to make known that the Holy One, blessed be He, is the third partner who breathes into his nostrils the breath of life — for according to the belief of those who assert primordial eternity, the Holy One, blessed be He, has no part in the creation of man. And this is the secret of the additional soul that keepers of the Sabbath possess on the day of Sabbath. And a proof for the matter, that they were both commanded at Marah, is from what is written in the latter tablets concerning Sabbath and concerning honoring father and mother: ״כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוְּךָ ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — as HaShem your Elohim has commanded you; and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 56b) ״כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוְּךָ״ — at Marah. And those who interpret hints said that the final letters of these four words add up to the numerical value of ״מָרָה״, for there they were commanded concerning them. And the red heifer and the civil laws are alluded to in what is written: ״חֹק וּמִשְׁפָּט״ — statute and ordinance — that is, the statute of the heifer, and ordinances and civil laws; and a hint to the matter: ״וְאֵלֶּה הַמִּשְׁפָּטִים אֲשֶׁר״ (Exodus 21:1) — the final letters spell ״מָרָה״. And ״פָרָה אֲשֶׁר אֵין בָּהּ מוּם״ — a cow that has no blemish — is a hint: take mem from ״פָּרָה״ and then ״מָרָה״ remains. And do not wonder that ״מוּם״ is written with a vav, for something similar to this Rabbi Yaakov Ba'al ha-Turim wrote in parashat Vayishlah (Genesis 32:15) ״עִזִּים מָאתַיִם״ etc. — the final letters of all of them are mem, because he sent him blemished animals that are not fit for sacrifice." "And the reason for this commandment is that through seeing that a bitter thing is healed by a bitter thing like itself, they would not question this commandment, that the red heifer defiles the pure and purifies the impure. And so explained the Ri Ba'al ha-Turim. And the reason for this commandment now: for through belief in creation anew there is a heavenly court above if there is no court below, as explained above in parashat Bereshit regarding what Rabbi Yitzhak said, that the Torah did not need to begin from Bereshit, etc., but rather so that the nations of the world should not say to you, 'You are thieves,' and there it is explained that this depends on that — see there."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:03.928383035Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}